#{[#edb/ref 17592186048835 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064979} :activity/id #uuid "8ea4ae18-ffc0-59bc-9ddc-fdba78903ea0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065394 :tutorial/content "Derivatives naturally occur in many real-world situations, so it's essential to know their units of measurement. The key is to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}?$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ measures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064152 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064035 :question/id #uuid "b7051104-6e23-5ab2-8779-5d54a3c3f5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-270" :question/problem "Consider a spherical balloon with radius $r.$ As the balloon loses air, its volume $V$ decreases. What is the best interpretation of $V'(r)?$\n\n![](images/7f/7f52288ca4670375413ecd5283bc2d15fbb332af311ead07986c1526d25e338a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $V(r)$ gives the volume of a sphere with radius $r.$ So\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}V}{\\textrm{d}r} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }V}{\\textrm{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $V'(r)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes."} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :question/id #uuid "8378ead2-9247-55a0-acc8-2e6d0874ea09" :question/math-academy-id "e-271" :question/problem "The function $B(s)$ models a school's budget, in dollars, given the average number of students $s$ in each grade. What is the best interpretation of $B'(30)=2\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $B'(s)$ represents the rate at which the budget is changing at the moment when the school has an average of exactly $s$ students in each grade.\n\nTherefore, $B'(30) = 2\\,000$ means:\n\n*When the school has an average of $30$ students in each grade, its budget is increasing at a rate of $2\\,000$ dollars per student.*"} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064037 :question/id #uuid "750e7350-1434-56ef-80d8-7cf531108f96" :question/math-academy-id "e-273" :question/problem "On a particular day, the rate $E$ at which tourists enter a small country can be modeled as $E(t) = 50\\cdot 3^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, after midnight. The rate at which tourists leave the country can be modeled as $L(t)=100\\cdot 2^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is also the time in hours after midnight.\n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the number of tourists in the country at time $t$ is equal to the entry rate minus the exit rate at time $t,$ that is, \n\n$$\nE(t)-L(t).\n$$\n\nSince $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is ten hours after midnight, we have $t=10.$ Substituting $t=10,$ the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(10)-L(10) & =50\\cdot {3}^{0.2(10)}-100\\cdot {2}^{0.2(10)} \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is $50$ tourists per hour."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064114 :question/id #uuid "5831f44c-02c6-5a5b-a9e7-58c7ac36cb6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6112" :question/problem "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064980}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064115 :question/id #uuid "0f4baedc-a28a-5b45-97d7-d7e1196b21c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6113" :question/problem "Howard, the manager of a sci-fi convention, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of visitors to the convention, $t$ minutes after the convention has opened.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the convention\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the convention\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the convention\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the convention $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}N(t)}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }N}{\\textrm{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $N'(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the convention changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the convention at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = E(t) - L(t).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}}] :activity/title "Interpreting the Meaning of the Derivative in Context" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064399}] [#edb/ref 17592186048826 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657} :activity/id #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075033 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 4; x - 2 = y\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 4 \\\\\nx + (x - 2) &= 4 \\\\\n2x - 2 &= 4 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3$. But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 2 \\\\\ny &= 3 - 2 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 1\\right)\n$$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x - y = 4; x + 2y = 16$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4 + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x+2y &=16 \\\\ (4+y) + 2y &=16 \\\\ 4+3y &=16 \\\\ 3y &=12 \\\\ y &=4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into\n\n$$\nx=4+y\n$$\n\n, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= 4 + y \\\\ &= 4 + (4) \\\\ &=8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${2u + 9v - 6 = 0; 2u + 6v - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n{2u + 9v - 6 = 0(1); 2u + 6v - 8 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+6v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 3v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2+3v &= 0 \\\\ 3v &= -2 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-12 &= 0 \\\\ 2u &= 12 \\\\ u &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073923}] [#edb/ref 17592186048896 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514} :activity/id #uuid "0c5859d1-4ff1-5efe-90ef-d19961e8bcc4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088889 :tutorial/content "In mathematics, rectangular arrays containing numbers are called **matrices**. \n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A$ below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis particular matrix has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. The numbers in the matrix are called **entries** or **elements.**\n\nThe number of rows and columns in a matrix does not need to be the same. For example, consider another matrix that has $3$ rows and $4$ columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 13 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe **dimensions** of the matrix are the number of rows and the number of columns. For example, the matrix $M$ shown above has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, we write the dimensions of $M$ as $3 \\times 4$ (read as \"three by four\")." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "Some matrices have only one column, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **column vectors**.\n\nOther matrices have only one row, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $D$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **row vectors**.\n\n**Note:** Every number can be thought of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix. For instance, we can consider the number $5$ as the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\,5\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/title "Matrices With One Row or Column"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088898 :tutorial/content "A **square matrix** is a matrix where the number of rows is equal to the number of columns.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a square matrix because it has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Its dimensions are $2 \\times 2.$\n\nIn general, the dimensions of a square matrix are $n \\times n.$" :tutorial/title "Square Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088984 :tutorial/content "Matrices arise naturally in *many* real-world problems. Weather forecasting, image processing and computer graphics, video games, and online customization algorithms are just a few of the many applications of matrices. \n\nLet's consider an example where we can use a matrix to model bus routes in a city. \n\nThe diagram below shows the bus routes between three stations (Station A, Station B, and Station C) in a particular city, where $A, B,$ and $C$ represent the stations and the arrows represent the directed routes between them. \n\n![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nNote that the circles $A,B,$ and $C$ in our diagram are called **nodes**.\n\nWe wish to represent this same diagram using a matrix. Let's start by putting this information into a table like the one below:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** |  |  |  |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nLet's now fill in the rows of our table. We begin by considering all of the bus routes from Station A to the other stations:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}0$ bus routes from $A$ to itself.\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $C.$\n\nFilling in the first row of the table, we get the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\color{blue} 0$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nFilling in the remaining rows in the same way, we get:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, keeping only the essential information, we obtain the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/title "Using Matrices for Modeling"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928}] [#edb/ref 17592186048813 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425} :activity/id #uuid "0693eaa1-2cbe-58a3-aa5d-c539aa74793e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{'}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096889}]}
